  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Multiplication, Division and Mixed Decimal Operations",
    "description": "Multiply and divide decimals, preserve scale, solve missing-term equations and evaluate mixed expressions.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 45,
    "teachingApproach": "Products and decimal scale → power-of-ten shifts → division methods → unknown terms → operation order and checks.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 3: operations rules and solved examples 8-16 on printed pages 72-73; objective patterns involving decimal products, quotients, missing terms and mixed expressions on printed pages 75-77 and subsequent exercise pages."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Decimal multiplication and division are reliable when you track scale explicitly. For a product, first multiply the digits as whole numbers and then count the decimal places in all factors. For division, distinguish a counting-number divisor from a decimal divisor; in the latter case, scale both dividend and divisor equally until the divisor is a whole number.\n\nWe will also use known products to infer scaled results, solve for a missing factor or divisor, and evaluate mixed expressions in the stated order. Estimation and inverse operations help catch misplaced decimal points.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "multiply_place_count"
        }
      ]
    },
    "multiply_place_count": {
      "id": "multiply_place_count",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To multiply terminating decimals, temporarily ignore their decimal points and multiply as whole numbers. Then count the decimal places in every factor; the product must have that total number of places. This works because each factor is a whole-number count of tenths, hundredths, or smaller units.\n\nFor 2.1693 × 1.4, multiply 21693 × 14 = 303702. The factors have four and one decimal places, so place five in the product: 3.03702. Check the size: a value near 2 multiplied by 1.4 should be near 3, not hundreds.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_multiply_place_count"
        }
      ]
    },
